# Shell JSON and reviews

## Prefer jq for JSON

When parsing or inspecting JSON from `gh`, APIs, or files in the shell, **prefer `jq`** (or `gh … --jq`) over Python/node one-liners so output is glanceable in the terminal.

```bash
# Good
gh issue list --limit 20 --json number,title,state \
  | jq -r '.[] | "\(.number)\t\(.state)\t\(.title)"'

gh pr view 22 --json title,state,url --jq '"\(.state)\t\(.title)\t\(.url)"'
```

Use Python only when the transform is awkward in jq. Prefer printing final tables via jq when practical.

## Reviews: normal by default; GAN when it matters

**Default review** = correctness, clarity, conventions (docs, plans, wiki, example polish). Not adversarial.

**GAN / adversarial review** (try to break the change) when:

1. The user explicitly asks for GAN / adversarial review, or
2. The diff is **merge-critical code**: library `*.go` (package root), `experiments/*/main.go`, or a PR about to merge that ships runnable behavior

Author walkthroughs only when the user asks for peer-code-review / walkthrough.

Follow `.agents/skills/review/SKILL.md` for mode selection and GAN output format.
